parts.text:
The edit is saved. So the resolution issue: Turbopack resolves `tw-animate-css/dist/tw-animate.css` — but the error still says "." not exported under node/require. Perhaps Turbopack's CSS @import resolution inside a transpiled package (theme.css from @capitech/ui) resolves bare imports from the *root* app's node_modules (where tw-animate-css may not be hoisted... wait, it IS installed: packages/ui/node_modules/tw-animate-css). Hmm.

Actually — maybe the problem is different: Tailwind v4's `@import` with bare specifiers resolves via its own import resolver (postcss-import style), which uses require conditions, and tw-animate-css only exports "style". Tailwind v4 supports the "style" condition for @import... it should. But the error indicates it tried conditions ["node","require"].

Look at the error more carefully: `C:\Ai Project\Capitech\packages\ui\node_modules\tw-animate-css` — so it IS resolving from packages/ui/node_modules. The condition list ["node","require"] fails.

Simplest bulletproof fix: don't depend on tw-animate-css at all. Remove the import and define the animations I use in theme.css myself. My components use: animate-in/animate-out + fade-in-0, fade-out-0, zoom-in-95, zoom-out-95, slide-in-from-*, slide-out-to-*, pulse (built-in), spin (built-in). These are Tailwind v4 built-ins? In Tailwind v4, `animate-in`, `fade-in`, `zoom-in`, `slide-in-from-*` are NOT built-in — they come from tw-animate-css/tailwindcss-animate. But `animate-pulse` and `animate-spin` ARE built-in.

So I need to hand-roll the animation utilities used across my components:
- fade-in-0 / fade-out-0
- zoom-in-95 / zoom-out-95
- slide-in-from-top/bottom/left/right-2, slide-out-to-*
- animate-in / animate-out (base)
- spin-in, rotate etc. not used.

Let me write a small custom CSS block in theme.css defining these utilities via Tailwind v4 `@utility` directives. That's clean and version-proof.

Tailwind v4 @utility syntax:
```css
@utility animate-in {
  animation-name: enter;
  animation-duration: 150ms;
  --tw-enter-opacity: initial;
  --tw-enter-scale: initial;
  --tw-enter-rotate: initial;
  --tw-enter-translate-x: initial;
  --tw-enter-translate-y: initial;
}
```

That's complex. Alternative simpler approach: define plain CSS classes with the same names:

```css
@layer utilities {
  .animate-in { animation: animate-in 0.15s ease-out; }
  ...
}
```

But the components use combos like `data-[state=open]:animate-in data-[state=closed]:animate-out data-[state=closed]:fade-out-0 data-[state=open]:fade-in-0` with Radix data-state. If I define simple CSS classes, they'd apply on data-state via the variant `data-[state=open]:` — that works in Tailwind (data attribute variant compiles to [data-state=open] selector).

But the transform utilities like `zoom-in-95`, `slide-in-from-top-2` need to compose into a single animation — in tailwindcss-animate they use CSS custom properties (--tw-enter-*) consumed by the animate-in keyframe. Composing via custom properties is the right approach.

Let me implement a mini version supporting what I use:

```css
@layer utilities {
  .animate-in {
    animation-name: tw-animate-enter;
    animation-duration: var(--tw-animate-duration, 150ms);
    animation-fill-mode: both;
    --tw-enter-opacity: initial;
    --tw-enter-scale: initial;
    --tw-enter-rotate: initial;
    --tw-enter-translate-x: initial;
    --tw-enter-translate-y: initial;
  }
  .animate-out {
    animation-name: tw-animate-exit;
    animation-duration: var(--tw-animate-duration, 150ms);
    animation-fill-mode: both;
    --tw-exit-opacity: initial;
    --tw-exit-scale: initial;
    --tw-exit-rotate: initial;
    --tw-exit-translate-x: initial;
    --tw-exit-translate-y: initial;
  }
  .fade-in-0 { --tw-enter-opacity: 0; }
  .fade-out-0 { --tw-exit-opacity: 0; }
  .zoom-in-95 { --tw-enter-scale: .95; }
  .zoom-out-95 { --tw-exit-scale: .95; }
  .slide-in-from-top-2 { --tw-enter-translate-y: -0.5rem; }
  .slide-in-from-bottom-2 { --tw-enter-translate-y: 0.5rem; }
  .slide-in-from-left-2 { --tw-enter-translate-x: -0.5rem; }
  .slide-in-from-right-2 { --tw-enter-translate-x: 0.5rem; }
  .slide-out-to-top-2 { --tw-exit-translate-y: -0.5rem; }
  ...
  @keyframes tw-animate-enter {
    from { opacity: var(--tw-enter-opacity, 1); transform: translate3d(var(--tw-enter-translate-x, 0), var(--tw-enter-translate-y, 0), 0) scale(var(--tw-enter-scale, 1)) rotate(var(--tw-enter-rotate, 0)); }
    to { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }
  @keyframes tw-animate-exit { ... }
}
```

Wait — but using these with data-[state=open]: variants: Tailwind's data variant generates `.data-\[state\=open\]\:animate-in[data-state="open"]`. Since these are custom classes in @layer utilities, the variant system won't generate them automatically — variants only apply to utilities Tailwind knows about. Custom classes in @layer utilities via plain CSS aren't variant-aware!

To make them variant-aware in Tailwind v4, use `@utility` directive:

```css
@utility animate-in { ... }
```

@utility makes the utility first-class so `data-[state=open]:animate-in` works. Let me write it with @utility for each. @utility supports nested? @utility requires the utility name and body; multiple utilities each need their own block.

Let me write:

```css
@utility animate-in {
  animation-name: tw-animate-enter;
  animation-duration: var(--tw-animate-duration, 150ms);
  animation-fill-mode: both;
  --tw-enter-opacity: initial;
  --tw-enter-scale: initial;
  --tw-enter-rotate: initial;
  --tw-enter-translate-x: initial;
  --tw-enter-translate-y: initial;
}
@utility fade-in-0 { --tw-enter-opacity: 0; }
...
@keyframes tw-animate-enter { ... }
```

The keyframes at top level of theme.css (outside @utility) get included in the output. Good.

I also use `animate-pulse` (built-in) and `animate-spin` (built-in) — fine.

Also `transition-all`, `duration-200` built-in. And in sonner/dialog I used `data-[state=open]:animate-in` etc. And `data-[state=closed]:duration-300` etc. Those are fine.

Let me also handle `zoom-in-95` and `zoom-out-95`, `fade-in-0`, `fade-out-0`, slide variants used: slide-in-from-top-2 (tabs? no, tooltip), slide-in-from-bottom-2, slide-in-from-left-2, slide-in-from-right-2 (select popper, sheet), slide-out-to-top/bottom/left/right (sheet). Plus `slide-in-from-top` (without -2) in sheet variants: I used `data-[state=open]:slide-in-from-top` — the base slide-in-from-top with no suffix should default to 1rem? In tailwindcss-animate, `slide-in-from-top` alone isn't valid — it's `slide-in-from-top-2` etc. Let me check my sheet.tsx: I wrote `data-[state=open]:slide-in-from-top` — that's invalid; should be `slide-in-from-top-2` or `slide-in-from-top-4`. Actually tailwindcss-animate supports `slide-in-from-top-1` through `slide-in-from-top-24`? Hmm, actually tw-animate-css supports `slide-in-from-top-*` with any number? The original tailwindcss-animate had fixed classes for -1..-24? No — it had `slide-in-from-top-1` .. using arbitrary values. The shadcn sheet uses `data-[state=open]:slide-in-from-top` — wait no, current shadcn sheet uses:
`data-[state=open]:slide-in-from-top-full` for top side? Let me recall: shadcn sheet.tsx variants:
```
top: "data-[state=open]:slide-in-from-top data-[state=closed]:slide-out-to-top inset-x-0 top-0 border-b"
```
Hmm and tw-animate-css defines `slide-in-from-top` without suffix? Let me check what tw-animate-css actually defines — read its CSS file! It's installed at packages/ui/node_modules/tw-animate-css/dist/tw-animate.css. Let me read it and mirror the exact utilities I need.

